/**
 * Computed signatures of the `onnx` module (C++ operators, modules/onnx).
 *
 * `supported_operators` and the pass form of `export_model` are declared in stdlib/onnx.cml. The
 * entry here is the call form, whose signature a declaration cannot write:
 *   - its first parameter is a function of any parameter list and result, which no declaration can
 *     name (the arbitrary-function-type gap);
 *   - its `example` parameter is a tuple with one value for each parameter of the function (a
 *     single value for a one-parameter function), and each leaf of it must be of the type of the
 *     parameter tree of the function -- a predicate on a type variable, which a declaration cannot
 *     state either.
 * The `DynamicFuncTypeResolver` of modules/onnx is the one source of this signature. `ExampleOf<F>`
 * below names the type the resolver computes; it is not a type a program can write.
 *
 * For reference only: these declarations are not compiled. They are checked against the operators
 * the module registers, and docs/stdlib-reference.md is generated from them.
 */

/// Writes the ONNX model of `f` applied to arguments like `example` to the file `out`, at the
/// backend's default opset. The function's graph is translated after the generic passes, at the
/// shapes of `example`; `dynamic_axes` leaves those axes of the tensor inputs unknown, and
/// `target` names the runtime whose capability data the model is checked against.
/// @fails error value `ExportUnsupported` when the graph cannot be translated (a call that remains, an operator without an ONNX lowering, a dtype or shape the exporter cannot name) or the target runtime rejects a node; error value `IOError` when the file cannot be written
/// @example-fragment the function and its example come from the surrounding program
/// @example export_model<out: '/tmp/model.onnx'>(net, x)
with <out: string, dynamic_axes: int[]? = null, target: string? = null>
export func export_model::<F>(f: F, example: ExampleOf<F>): void! uses fs when is_function::<F>()
